# Articles and Labs. Conceptual only. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([1], "What is the phonological similarity effect?", "Lists of similar-sounding items are recalled worse than lists of different-sounding items, even when the items are only read silently. Acoustic similarity effect is arguably the better name."),
 ([1], "Why does the phonological similarity effect show a speech-based code?", "People recode what they see into a speech-based form. A purely visual code would cause mix-ups between look-alikes such as E and F instead."),
 ([1], "How does the phonological loop explain the effect, and what does articulatory suppression do?", "The loop has a phonological store and an articulatory control process that converts visual input to speech and refreshes traces. Articulatory suppression occupies that process, and with visually presented lists the similarity cost disappears."),
 ([2], "What is a word’s neighborhood?", "It is the set of words that differ from it by one letter, orthographic, or one sound, phonological. Cat is a large neighborhood word and hippopotamus a small neighborhood word."),
 ([2], "What did Roodenrys et al. (2002) find?", "Large neighborhood words are recalled better in short-term memory tasks, so long-term memory knowledge of words helps short-term recall."),
 ([2], "Why is memory span not a pure measure of short-term capacity?", "Because long-term memory knowledge of words affects short-term recall, so span reflects more than short-term capacity."),
 ([3], "What is the Brown–Peterson task?", "People study consonants and then do a distractor task, counting backwards, that blocks rehearsal. The longer the distraction, the worse the recall."),
 ([3], "What did Peterson and Peterson (1959) conclude?", "Because counting is so unlike remembering letters, they judged interference an unlikely cause and concluded that short-term memory loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed."),
 ([3], "What view did the conclusion of Peterson and Peterson (1959) challenge?", "It challenged the view, following McGeoch’s (1932) critique, that forgetting is due to interference. Decay is the researchers’ conclusion, and Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for an alternative."),
 ([4], "What is the modality effect?", "Memory is better for the last one or two items of a list when it is heard than when it is read silently, and lip-read or silently mouthed lists show it too."),
 ([4], "How did the modality effect influence ideas about sensory memory?", "It strongly influenced ideas about sensory memory, a back-up store of raw, unanalyzed sensory input."),
 ([4], "What explanation does Neath and Surprenant (2003) favor?", "Hearing adds extra information about how the list sounds, which helps the final items."),
 ([5], "What did Sperling (1960) argue about the limit on reporting a brief display?", "That the limit comes from recall and not from perception."),
 ([5], "What does partial report show in Sperling (1960)?", "In Sperling (1960), a cue right after the display lets observers report the cued part almost perfectly, so a sensory, iconic, store briefly held nearly all of it."),
 ([5], "What is the real limit on reporting a brief display in Sperling’s (1960) work?", "The time needed to report. If the cue is delayed, performance falls toward the usual limit, because the store is very short-lived."),
 ([6], "What is the position error effect?", "Items are usually recalled in the correct position, and when they are not, the errors are systematic, with the item landing in an adjacent position."),
 ([6], "Why do order errors matter for scoring memory?", "Order errors are not random, and scoring only percent correct hides that people keep approximate position information."),
 ([7], "Why are self-reports of mental imagery unreliable?", "It is impossible to tell whether the images differ or only the descriptions, so researchers use tasks that require imagery and vary a critical part of the task."),
 ([7], "What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) have people do?", "They had people judge whether two block shapes were the same or mirror images, with one turned."),
 ([7], "What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) find, and what does it suggest?", "People mentally rotated one shape, and the larger the turn, the longer the response, at a constant rate. This suggests mental images are much like real images, and it influenced theories of mental representation."),
 ([8], "What is the typical way people judge probability?", "With heuristics that trade accuracy for speed, which are usually good enough but sometimes wrong. Tversky and Kahneman (1983) showed estimates often differ greatly from objective probability."),
 ([8], "What is the conjunction fallacy?", "Judging that A and B together are more likely than A alone or B alone, although a combination is never more likely than either part alone."),
 ([8], "What did Shafir, Smith and Osherson (1990) find?", "People sometimes rated a combination of professions or activities as more likely than one part, which points to heuristics rather than probability rules."),
 ([9], "In the Wason Selection Task, which cards best test the rule If a card has a D on one side, it has a 3 on the other?", "D and 7, since the 7 could falsify the rule. Most people choose D and 3, and the 3 can only confirm the rule."),
 ([9], "How is the same logic easier in a familiar thematic rule?", "With the rule If you borrow my car, you must fill up the gas tank, the correct cards are borrowed car and empty gas tank."),
 ([9], "What did Wason and Shapiro (1971) compare?", "Abstract and thematic rules. People test a rule well with relevant experience, and abstract rules force them to use logic alone."),
 ([10], "What are framing effects?", "They occur when the wording or context makes a big difference to a choice, even for otherwise equivalent situations. Kahneman and Tversky (1982) summarized many of them."),
 ([10], "How does framing change a choice in classic Tversky and Kahneman research?", "Gain wording typically leads to the sure option and loss wording to the gamble, so choices follow the framing and not the substance."),
 ([11], "What is the Monty Hall 3 Door problem?", "After you pick one of three doors, the host, who always plays fair this way, opens a no-prize door."),
 ([11], "Should you stay or switch in the Monty Hall problem, and why?", "You should switch, which wins about two-thirds of the time. Your first pick keeps its 1 in 3 chance, so the 2 in 3 held by the other two doors moves to the one remaining door."),
 ([11], "What does the Monty Hall problem show about intuition?", "It shows poor intuition about the probability of combinations of events, since many people think stay or switch makes no difference."),
 ([12], "What are risky decisions?", "Choices between a guaranteed outcome and a gamble, where the guaranteed outcome can be a gain or a loss, and the choice depends on how outcomes are framed and perceived."),
 ([12], "What does prospect-theory research of Kahneman and Tversky show about risk?", "People typically avoid risk with gains, taking the sure thing, and seek risk with losses, taking the gamble."),
 ([12], "Why do decisions about risk not depend only on the odds?", "They depend on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses, not only on the objective odds."),
 ([13], "What did German and Defeyter (2000) test?", "Whether young children get stuck on an object’s usual function, functional fixedness, using a box that was first shown as a container."),
 ([13], "What did German and Defeyter (2000) find?", "Older children, 6 and 7, were slower to use the box as a support after seeing it as a container, while 5-year-olds were not slowed and were even faster."),
 ([13], "What does German and Defeyter’s (2000) result suggest about young children?", "Younger children seem to have a more fluid idea of function, so one demonstrated use did not lock the object in."),
 ([14], "What is the irrelevant speech effect in Leist and colleagues (2022)?", "Speech to be ignored impairs serial recall, more for changing-state speech than for steady-state speech."),
 ([14], "What did Leist and colleagues (2022) find about verbal and spatial items?", "Speech hurt verbal recall but not spatial recall, in children and adults."),
 ([14], "Which accounts does Leist and colleagues’ (2022) result fit better than attention-capture or changing-state accounts?", "It fits phonological interference better, because the effect is specific to verbal material."),
 ([15], "What is cognitive offloading in Morrison and Richmond (2020)?", "Using actions like writing things down to reduce mental demands, as described by Risko and Gilbert (2016)."),
 ([15], "What did Morrison and Richmond (2020) find?", "Offloading improved memory, especially at high loads, and people offloaded more as load grew."),
 ([15], "Did memory ability predict who offloaded in Morrison and Richmond (2020)?", "No. Memory ability did not predict who offloaded, so offloading may be a compensatory strategy across abilities."),
 ([16], "What is the bat-and-ball problem (Frederick, 2005)?", "A bat and ball cost $1.10 and the bat costs $1.00 more. The intuitive answer is 10 cents, and the correct answer is 5 cents."),
 ([16], "What did Kahneman and Frederick (2002) and De Neys and colleagues (2013) say about the error?", "Kahneman and Frederick called the error unconscious attribute substitution, and De Neys and colleagues found wrong reasoners were less confident than on a control version."),
 ([16], "What did Hoover and Healy (2019) find?", "Wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than, and because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error, they call it a sensitivity error."),
 ([17], "What two effects did Giuliani and colleagues (2023) combine?", "The framing effect, a sure option for gains and a risky option for losses, and the default effect, choosing the preselected option."),
 ([17], "What did Giuliani and colleagues (2023) find about defaults and framing?", "A risky default raised risk taking, a sure default did not raise risk aversion, and the two effects were additive."),
 ([17], "What did the exploratory question show in Giuliani and colleagues (2023)?", "In an exploratory question asked after the choice, the belief about the default’s origin went with following or rejecting it."),
 ([18], "What do Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013) argue about adolescent risk taking?", "Adolescents reason about risk like adults in the lab, but the peer context is what raises risk taking."),
 ([18], "What did Chein and colleagues (2011) find in their driving game?", "Adolescents took more risks when watched by friends, with greater reward-region activity."),
 ([18], "Did peers change cognitive control in Chein and colleagues (2011)?", "No. Cognitive-control regions did not change with peers, so the authors concluded the effect worked mainly through reward circuitry."),
]
R = [
 ("How do the phonological similarity effect and the Brown–Peterson task both concern short-term memory but different questions?", "The similarity effect shows a speech-based code in the phonological loop, and the Brown–Peterson task asks how short-term memory loses information when rehearsal is blocked.", [1,3]),
 ("How do decay and interference differ as explanations of forgetting in the Brown–Peterson discussion?", "Peterson and Peterson (1959) judged interference an unlikely cause because counting is so unlike remembering letters, and concluded that short-term memory loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed. This challenged the view, following McGeoch’s (1932) critique, that forgetting is due to interference, and Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for an alternative.", [3]),
 ("How do the neighborhood size effect of Roodenrys et al. (2002) and the similarity effect each show about short-term recall?", "Neighborhood size shows that long-term word knowledge helps short-term recall, so span is not a pure measure. The similarity effect shows the speech-based code used in short-term memory.", [1,2]),
 ("How do the modality effect and Sperling’s (1960) work both bear on sensory memory?", "The modality effect influenced ideas about a raw sensory back-up store, and partial report showed an iconic store briefly holds nearly all of a display.", [4,5]),
 ("How do the position error effect and percent-correct scoring relate?", "Percent correct treats misplaced items as simply wrong, while errors landing in adjacent positions show people keep approximate position information.", [6]),
 ("How does the mental rotation result of Shepard and Metzler (1971) show that imagery can be studied without self-report?", "In Shepard and Metzler (1971), response time rose at a constant rate with the angle of turn, so a measurable pattern supports image-like representations.", [7]),
 ("How do heuristics, the conjunction fallacy and framing share an idea about judgment?", "Each shows judgments following a shortcut or wording and not objective probability or substance.", [8,10,12]),
 ("How do the Wason Selection Task and the Monty Hall problem both show intuition going wrong?", "People choose cards that confirm the rule and think switching makes no difference, because they miss what falsifies or how probability shifts.", [9,11]),
 ("Why are thematic rules easier than abstract rules in the Wason Selection Task of Wason and Shapiro (1971)?", "With relevant experience people test a rule well, and abstract rules force them to use logic alone.", [9]),
 ("How do framing in decisions and risky decisions relate?", "Both show choices depending on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses, with the sure option for gains and the gamble for losses.", [10,12]),
 ("How does Giuliani and colleagues (2023) extend the classic framing result?", "It combined framing with defaults, and found a risky default raised risk taking while the two effects were additive.", [10,12,17]),
 ("How do German and Defeyter (2000) relate to functional fixedness and age?", "Functional fixedness is getting stuck on an object’s usual function, and younger children showed immunity to it, so one demonstrated use did not lock the object in.", [13]),
 ("How do Leist and colleagues (2022) and the phonological similarity effect both point to phonological processing?", "Irrelevant speech hurt verbal but not spatial recall, and similar-sounding items are recalled worse, so both depend on speech-based processes.", [1,14]),
 ("How does cognitive offloading in Morrison and Richmond (2020) relate to memory load and ability?", "Offloading helped most at high loads and increased as load grew, and ability did not predict who did it, so it may be a compensatory strategy.", [15]),
 ("What do heuristics and the bat-and-ball problem both say about wrong answers that feel natural?", "Heuristics trade accuracy for speed and are usually good enough but sometimes wrong. In the bat-and-ball problem the intuitive answer is 10 cents and the correct answer is 5 cents.", [8,16]),
 ("How do Hoover and Healy (2019) change the explanation of the bat-and-ball error?", "They found wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than, and that attribute substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause, so they call it a sensitivity error.", [16]),
 ("How do Chein and colleagues (2011) and Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013) fit together?", "Adolescents reason like adults in the lab, but when watched by friends they take more risks with greater reward-region activity, and the authors concluded the effect worked mainly through reward circuitry and not changed cognitive control.", [18]),
 ("How do Giuliani and colleagues (2023) and Chein and colleagues (2011) show context shaping risky choice?", "A risky default raised risk taking, framing flips sure and risky options, and peers raised adolescent risk taking, so context and not only reasoning changes risk.", [10,17,18]),
 ("How do the conjunction fallacy and the Wason Selection Task each show people departing from rules of probability or logic?", "In the conjunction fallacy people judge A and B together as more likely than A alone, although a combination is never more likely than either part. In the Wason Selection Task most people choose D and 3, and the 3 can only confirm the rule, while D and 7 test it.", [8,9]),
 ("How do the Brown–Peterson task and Sperling’s (1960) partial report each use a delay or a distractor to study memory?", "In the Brown–Peterson task a counting distractor blocks rehearsal and longer distraction gave worse recall. In Sperling’s (1960) partial report, delaying the cue made performance fall toward the usual limit, because the store is very short-lived.", [3,5]),
]
